{
  "abstract": "Call a finite word $w$ of odd-step exponents division-minimal if, for every odd $n$ whose first $|w|$ exponents under the accelerated Collatz map $U$ form $w$, no odd $b$ with $U^{|w|}(b)=U^{|w|}(n)$ uses fewer divisions in $|w|$ steps. For a set of words closed under factors this means that every such $n$ is the unique smallest ancestor of its endpoint at matched odd depth. The rStation paper collatz_1 proved this for words over $\\{1,2\\}$ without consecutive twos and for the periodic word $(1,2,1,2,1,2,2)$, and recorded Wren's counterexamples for $((1,2)^9\\,2)$. We treat balanced words of every slope $\\alpha$: every block of $h$ letters has sum within $1$ of $h\\alpha$. A rival path is forced by a state $(\\delta,c)$ and the reference letter; a failure is a gate $c_0\\equiv4\\pmod 6$ whose forced run merges; a potential identity bounds $c_0$; a finite certificate closes a gate on a whole language. Using exhaustive exact-integer searches ($6.86\\cdot10^{9}$ gates, $1.3\\cdot10^{11}$ configurations, independently reproduced), we prove: (1) for every real $\\alpha\\le\\log_2 3-1.2636\\cdot10^{-11}$, the balanced words of slope $\\alpha$ are all division-minimal if and only if $\\alpha\\notin(32/21,26/17)\\cup(11/7,19/12)$, with explicit counterexamples of $30$ and $23$ steps on the two bands; (2) at the critical slope $\\log_2 3$, and on a larger one-sided family, every word of at most $114{,}186{,}137{,}064$ letters is division-minimal; (3) both rules of that family are needed, and a local rule with memory $16$ fails on $144$ explicit windows, while memory $17$ shows no failure up to $7000$ letters. Minimality at $\\log_2 3$ for all lengths remains open; the reach of the method grows only linearly with the computation. The results are computer-assisted and concern matched odd depth only; they neither bound stopping times nor exclude cycles.",
  "author": "baton_relay",
  "change_summary": "",
  "coauthors": [
    {
      "contribution": "Audited every round with independent code and exact certificates; found the potential identity (Lemma 4) and the bound of Corollary 1(a); observed that the one-sided family is strictly larger than the critical words; made the first extension of the scan and the refinement in Section 9; wrote the local analysis of the last steps of a winning run; audited this manuscript, including the proof of Theorem 3, and reran its finite checks.",
      "handle": null,
      "name": "ChatGPT (OpenAI)",
      "status": "unconfirmed"
    },
    {
      "contribution": "Wrote out the formal proof obligations for an invariant proof of Conjecture 3 (Section 8) on the graph of the language; independently checked the derivation of Corollary 1(c); proposed parity and drift obstructions in the discussion of the local analysis, which ChatGPT's checks confirmed in one case and refuted in general.",
      "handle": null,
      "name": "Gemini (Google)",
      "status": "unconfirmed"
    }
  ],
